.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt PORTING 7
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm porting
.Nd host cross-development for the bounded native C interface
.Sh DESCRIPTION
ABSD applications are static x86-64 ABSD ELF executables, not Linux
binaries.
The delivered C interface uses the native runtime, Picolibc 1.8.12, and the
C kit's descriptor adapter over explicit handles.
An executable receives only the startup record's arguments, environment,
working directory, streams and filesystem binding.
Paths and C descriptors do not confer ambient authority.
See
.Xr handles 7
and
.Xr process 7 .
.Pp
Development runs on an Ubuntu 24.04 x86-64 host using the matching staged
SDK and host clang/LLVM prerequisites.
There is no compiler, assembler, linker, development-header or library
installation in the operator image.
No tested general Rust application recipe is supplied here.
The SDK is a local engineering candidate: full archive/header licence
qualification and public distribution remain owner-held.
Do not infer a download or stable ABI promise from this reference.
.Ss Input, output and status
Use main's argc/argv or open named files for C input.
The cited #121 SDK examples exercise arguments and named files, not C
standard input.
At the descriptor layer, an open descriptor 0 delegates reads to the
runtime's startup input stream; no input handle yields end of file, and a
closed descriptor or invalid handle can yield EBADF.
The earlier Develop report's stdin EBADF finding is not a blanket adapter
refusal or qualification of other startup-stream arrangements.
C standard output and standard error use the granted output streams.
Return main's status to report success or failure; check library errors
rather than interpreting partial output as success.
Named files are limited by the process's binding and its read/write rights.
For accepted file-server changes and durability, see
.Xr sync 1
and
.Xr filesystem 7 .
.Pp
The profile is static and single-task.
It is not a POSIX conformance claim: unsupported process, signal, thread,
loader and filesystem facilities are absent or refused as specified in the
matching C interface contract.
Optional C++0 has a separate bounded profile and acceptance record;
exceptions, application RTTI, true threads, iostreams and std::filesystem
are not provided by it.
.Ss Host workflow
The matching platform source's
.Pa docs/HOST-SDK.md
is the canonical staged-SDK guide;
.Pa c/README.md
covers building and using the C kit from source, and
.Pa docs/C-INTERFACE.md
owns the complete C contract and refusal semantics.
Use the SDK manifest's tested kernel/platform pair and actual build-source
identity, not an unrelated runtime or host library.
.Pp
On the development host, save this argument-only example as words.c:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    size_t bytes = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i < argc; ++i) bytes += strlen(argv[i]);
    printf("words=%d bytes=%zu\en", argc - 1, bytes);
    return 0;
}
.Ed
.Pp
After verifying the supplied archive and SDK:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
"$SDK/bin/absd-sdk-check"
"$SDK/bin/absd-cc" -O2 -o words words.c
"$SDK/bin/absd-check-elf" words --map words.map
.Ed
.Pp
The invocation below prints words=2 bytes=9.
It uses argc/argv rather than stdin.
Keep the generated map and toolchain records; rebuild after relocating the
SDK because the map names the linked archives.
The ELF checker checks the actual image/link form, not Linux executability.
.Pp
Upload from the development host over the operator-provided pinned SSH
connection using binary-safe cat transport:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ssh -i ./operator-key -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -o IdentityAgent=none \e
    -o ForwardAgent=no -o UserKnownHostsFile=./absd-known-hosts \e
    -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes -p "$PORT" operator@"$HOST" \e
    'cat > /home/operator/words' < words
ssh -i ./operator-key -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -o IdentityAgent=none \e
    -o ForwardAgent=no -o UserKnownHostsFile=./absd-known-hosts \e
    -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes -p "$PORT" operator@"$HOST" \e
    '/home/operator/words apple pear'
.Ed
.Pp
HOST and PORT, operator-key and absd-known-hosts must identify the
operator-supplied ABSD destination and credentials, as specified in the
host SDK guide.
Do not request a terminal for the binary transfer.
Matching operator images also provide the bounded SFTP version 3 subsystem
for ordinary OpenSSH
.Nm sftp
and default modern
.Nm scp
uploads and downloads through the same pinned connection.
Individual reads and writes are limited to 64 KiB; write ranges and
size updates are limited to 64 MiB.
Creation permissions are advisory; permission, ownership and timestamp
preservation, APPEND, link operations and vendor extensions are unsupported.
An interrupted upload may leave a partial file.
See
.Xr absd-sftp 8
for the supported requests, remaining limits and refusal semantics.
Direct absolute-path execution works on the compatible development pair.
Change the program, rebuild, check, upload and rerun to confirm the new
output; retain actual status and pair identities on failure.
.Ss Existing execution evidence
The independently reviewed #121 outsider proof on November 3, 2026 used
runtime source c940d162c73dbce842ac37180940f14bc5e03a01 and kernel
deb92457144cbe7ea1a12e768bfba2e9c83a6fb9.
Its independent Ubuntu 24.04 write/build/upload/run/edit/rerun workflow
recorded eight exact Linux/guest comparisons across C and C++0, with
historical C++0 checked separately.
See the engineering handoff
.No "#121 local SDK candidate evidence" .
This is evidence for those programs and that pair, not a rerun of this
manual or a public SDK distribution acceptance.
